{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And there were men, etc.</b> One needs to know why it uses a singular form for the plural, and further why it was necessary to say <b>and they could not</b> perform the Pesach after it already informed us that they were impure? Perhaps it is because, since HaShem commanded from the New Moon concerning the Pesach, as they explained in Pesaḥim (6a), that the statement ״וְיַעֲשׂוּ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶת הַפָּסַח״ was on the New Moon of Nisan, it would have been fitting for them to guard themselves from impurity, and reason would come to cast aspersion upon them, that they did not keep the commandment of HaShem and became impure and were not careful about the commandment of Pesach — therefore Scripture says <b>וַיְהִי</b> in the singular, to say that there was only a single matter of impurity in which these men became impure, and this very matter of impurity was not in their hands to guard against, and they became impure against their will, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Sukkah 25a) — whether according to Rabbi Yosi HaGelili who said they were bearers of the coffin of Yosef, whether according to Rabbi Akiva, whether according to Rabbi Yitzchak who said they were those impure through a met mitzvah who were required to become impure even though they knew that their impurity lasts through the fourteenth, and this is what the statement <b>and they could not</b> perform the Pesach alludes to, to say that they could not guard themselves from the impurity in order to perform the Pesach. And its saying <b>on that day</b>, our Sages of blessed memory already examined (Pesaḥim 90a) according to each one's opinion. It further intends by using the singular form, to say that because they were individuals, that is why they could not perform the Pesach; but if they had been a community, they could have performed the Pesach, for a community may offer in impurity. I subsequently saw in the Sifrei from our Sages of blessed memory that they made this same fine point." "<b>For a human soul.</b> Its saying ״for a human soul״ is to say: but for one impure through a sheretz they are able, according to their words of blessed memory (Pesaḥim 90a) who said that they may slaughter and sprinkle the blood on behalf of one impure through a sheretz, and he immerses and eats his Pesach in the evening; but one impure through a corpse — even if his seventh day falls on the fourteenth, they do not slaughter on his behalf, and this is its saying ״on that day.״ And Rabbi Akiva said, and this is his language: that their seventh day fell on the eve of Pesach, as it is written ״on that day״ — that is when they are unable, but on the day after it they would have been able. And also according to the opinion of the one who says ״on that day״ they became impure, there is room for our explanation in the statement ״for a human soul,״ to say that if it had been a sheretz, they would have slaughtered on his behalf." "<b>And they drew near before Moshe, etc.</b> The reason for their drawing near before Moshe and Aharon, according to the plain sense of the verse, is so that the ruling would come from Moshe immediately and directly to the priest, to offer their sacrifices if he so ruled. And our Sages of blessed memory in tractate Bava Batra, chapter Yesh Noḥalin (Bava Batra 119a) said, and this is their language: ״Is it possible that they stood before Moshe and he did not say, etc.? Rather, emend the verse and expound it״ — the words of Rabbi Yoshiyah. Abba Ḥanan said in the name of Rabbi Eliezer: ״They were sitting in the study hall and they came, etc.,״ end of quote. And they said in the Gemara, and this is their language: ״What do they disagree about? One Master holds that one accords honor to the student in the place of the teacher, and one Master holds that one does not accord honor. And the halakhah is: one accords honor, and the halakhah is: one does not accord honor — and this is not difficult: this is where his teacher accorded him honor; this is where his teacher did not accord him honor,״ end of their language. Now, from the fact that they said ״the halakhah is one accords honor״ and ״the halakhah is one does not accord honor,״ and they established their statement ״the halakhah is one accords honor״ where his teacher accorded him honor, and ״the halakhah is one does not accord honor״ where his teacher did not accord him honor — the matter proves that Rabbi Yoshiyah and Abba Ḥanan disagree concerning the commandments: one Master holds that one accords honor even when his teacher does not accord him honor, and one Master holds that one does not accord honor even when his teacher does accord him honor, for only in this way is it justified to say ״the halakhah is…״ and ״the halakhah is…״ — implying that they disagree." "<b>And we have</b> grounds to investigate this: from where does the Talmud have a basis to establish this dispute, that they disagree concerning the commandments, and all the more so given that it is the opposite of the general rule we apply, that we do not establish a dispute among our Sages of blessed memory concerning the commandments? And if the reason is that it was not known whether their dispute is where the teacher accords honor, etc., or where he does not accord honor, etc., there is no decisive basis to say that ״one does not accord honor״ applies only where his teacher does not accord him honor, etc., but when his teacher accords him honor, etc., they concede that one does accord honor; or we should go in this direction, that the Tannaim hold ״one does not accord honor״ only when his teacher accords him honor, etc., but when his teacher does not accord honor they concede that one does not accord him honor — and therefore the Talmud established a dispute concerning the commandments? This is difficult, for the Talmud should have established the straightforward meaning of the Tannaim's words first, according to the Talmud's customary procedure, and revealed the locus of their dispute, and only afterward said ״the halakhah is…״, etc." "<b>And it would appear to say</b> that the Talmud made the fine point that they disagree concerning the commandments from within their dispute itself, based on the very fact that they disagree — for if you say that they disagree concerning the commandments, well and good; but if they disagree only about one matter and agree about its counterpart, why does the Tanna disagree with his colleague's words, since he could establish his position according to the matter in which his colleague concedes? This too is insufficient, for this reason works with the words of Abba Ḥanan, who disagrees concerning the commandments even where his teacher does not accord him honor; but as for Rabbi Yoshiyah — who can say that he holds ״one does not accord honor״ even where his teacher does accord him honor? And furthermore, even regarding the one who disagrees, I ask: who revealed to him that the first Tanna, in all the commandments, is expressing this view?" "<b>However,</b> the explanation is this: from the fact that Rabbi Yehoshiah needed to emend the verse and interpret it contrary to the plain sense of the text, this indicates that he found no way to establish it without emendation. And if Rabbi Yehoshiah held that one does show honor to a student in the place of the teacher, he should have left the verse according to its plain sense and established it as referring to a case where his teacher shows him honor. And this would be entirely fitting in the case of the man Moses our master, upon him be peace, who was exceedingly humble, and who would show honor to Aaron his brother — all the more so since he was older than him. Further, we find that he did show him honor, as they said of blessed memory (Mekhilta, parashat Bo 12) that when they stood before Pharaoh, Moses would show honor to Aaron to let him speak first, and Aaron to Moses, and the speech would go forth from between the two of them. See, he did show him honor." "<b>And from the fact</b> that he emended the verse, he revealed his opinion that he holds that one does not show honor, and so forth, even when his teacher shows him honor. And from the fact that we find that they likewise disagreed concerning the matter of the daughters of Zelophehad, as it is written: ״וַתַּעֲמֹדְנָה לִפְנֵי מֹשֶׁה וְלִפְנֵי אֶלְעָזָר״ and so forth (Numbers 27:2) — and this is their language: 'It is possible that they stood before Moses' and so forth, 'and before Eleazar' — and it does not say' and so forth 'וַיַּעַמְדוּ לִפְנֵי הַנְּשִׂיאִים בָּעֵדָה וְהָעֵדָה' and so forth; rather, he emended the verse and interpreted it — the words of Rabbi Yehoshiah. Abba Ḥanan says in the name of Rabbi Eliezer: they were sitting in the study hall and so forth — and the Talmud brings the very same matter that we brought above. Now, from the fact that we see that Abba Ḥanan disagreed regarding the standing of the daughters of Zelophehad before the princes and the congregation, presumably the princes would not show honor to the entire congregation, and needless to say Moses, king of Israel — and even so, Abba Ḥanan said that they did show them honor. From this you learn that he holds that one does show honor even when his teacher does not show him honor. And from this we have now found a sound explanation for why Rabbi Yehoshiah and Abba Ḥanan disagreed twice."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:07:29.165344132Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}